1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,259
Three months ago, Lin Wei had imagined university as a quiet place where success would depend on private effort.

2
00:00:07,259 --> 00:00:17,992
Yet on a bright autumn morning, he stood outside Harbor City University's registration hall with a cell phone in one hand and a paper map in the other.

3
00:00:17,992 --> 00:00:26,125
The university had recently released an orientation app, but it kept sending students to a locked door.

4
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:30,665
Lately, Lin had tried to appear calm and youthful.

5
00:00:30,665 --> 00:00:36,249
Now he watched a creative crowd invent its own directions.

6
00:00:36,249 --> 00:00:46,427
“Good lord, there are plenty of maps, but none of them agree,” said Maya Chen, who was eating a noodle from a paper cup.

7
00:00:46,427 --> 00:00:53,876
A visitor nearby waved an unlit cigar and complained that the smoggy air made every building look alike.

8
00:00:53,876 --> 00:01:04,704
On one map, a grassy path crossed land marked for drought research; on another, the same path ended beside a tidal laboratory.

9
00:01:04,704 --> 00:01:10,344
The campus signs did little to illustrate which route was open.

10
00:01:10,344 --> 00:01:13,778
Then the registration kiosk broke.

11
00:01:13,778 --> 00:01:28,270
Its screen automatically moved upward through a list of error messages, including one called “address normalization.” A picky administrator told everyone to conform to the printed instructions.

12
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:35,630
Maya gave an exclamatory reply—“How?”—and asked Lin to summarize the problem.

13
00:01:35,630 --> 00:01:45,308
In language class, an infinitive might be easy to identify; in a crowd, “to register” was harder to achieve.

14
00:01:45,308 --> 00:01:52,207
Lin used the maps to illustrate the contradiction instead of arguing about blame.

15
00:01:52,207 --> 00:02:00,936
A volunteer stretched a rope across the unsafe path, and the line of students moved steadily toward the wrong building.

16
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:03,480
Some looked helpless.

17
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:10,254
Lin expected Maya to brag about finding the error first, but she was supportive.

18
00:02:10,254 --> 00:02:15,869
Together they made a simple sign, then added photographs to the app.

19
00:02:15,869 --> 00:02:21,473
Each clear image could strengthen one instruction and reinforce the next.

20
00:02:21,473 --> 00:02:26,187
Their correction worked with surprising rapidity.

21
00:02:26,187 --> 00:02:33,001
The old maps were theirs to criticize, but the new solution would be theirs to maintain.

22
00:02:33,001 --> 00:02:46,454
Near a wall of remembrance, under a starry design painted by former students, Maya said that a useful system should inspire confidence rather than reward the people who excel at guessing.

23
00:02:46,454 --> 00:02:50,368
Lin admitted that silence would have been cowardice.

24
00:02:50,368 --> 00:02:58,862
A boastful solution could not dignify its users, and technology alone could not civilize an institution.

25
00:02:58,862 --> 00:03:09,205
Their first design had to be adaptive: it should learn from mistakes, purify bad data, and respect people who needed help.

26
00:03:09,205 --> 00:03:15,292
That small decision became the beginning of their shared project.
